        "Why is 'decision' given so much importance in behavioural geography?",
        "Because humans act towards the environment through their decisions. Decisions decide the direction of human actions. Behavioural geography gives central importance to 'decision' because people living in the same environment take different decisions and get different results. Example — on similar land in the same village, one farmer grows wheat and another grows vegetables and earns more; the difference is not of land but of decision. Man's decisions depend on his experience, perception, information and values. Hence, to understand the environment one must understand man's decision-making process — this is the core logic of behavioural geography. Here man is the 'active agent' who chooses his response to environmental stimuli according to his perception.",
