        "Explain the three stages of the demographic transition theory.",
        "Stage 1: both birth and death rates are high — population stays almost stable. Stage 2: births stay high but deaths fall — population grows fast. Stage 3: both birth and death rates are low — population is stable or grows very slowly. The demographic transition theory explains how the rate of population growth changes with economic development. First stage — high birth and high death rates, stable population (like India before 1921). Second stage — death rate falls fast with medical care and sanitation, but birth rate stays high — population explosion occurs (present-day India is in this stage). Third stage — birth rate also falls with education and urbanisation; both rates low — population stable again (like Japan, Western Europe). The theory tells policy-makers that development itself brings population stability.",
