        "Three parts: birth rate (births per thousand people), death rate (deaths per thousand) and migration (coming and going). Natural growth = births − deaths; total growth also includes migration. The three components of population growth are birth rate, death rate and migration. Birth rate (live births per 1000 persons in a year) increases population; death rate (deaths per 1000) decreases it. Natural growth = birth rate − death rate — migration is not counted because it is a 'natural' (biological) process. Total/actual growth = natural growth ± net migration (in-migration − out-migration). If birth rate is 20 and death rate 7 (per 1000), natural growth is 13 per 1000, i.e. 1.3%. Migration changes an area's population immediately — e.g. in-migration to Gulf countries.",
        "long"
