        "Explain Weber's least cost theory.",
        "According to Alfred Weber, an industry is set up where the total cost of bringing raw material and sending finished goods is the lowest. Wage rates and clustering of industries (agglomeration) also affect it. Alfred Weber (German economist) gave the 'least cost theory' of industrial location in 1909. According to it, an industry is set up where the total transport cost is minimum — transport cost has two parts: the cost of bringing raw material to the factory and the cost of sending finished goods to the market. Industries using heavy, weight-losing raw material (like iron ore) locate near the raw material source; market-based ones (like bakeries) near the market. Weber also added the effects of labour cost and agglomeration. Criticism — the theory over-emphasises transport and neglects modern factors (policy, technology).",
